WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.968
 

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:05.412
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.412 --> 00:00:14.290
 

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:17.000
DAVID ADJAYE: Good architecture
does have this great propensity

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.880
to amplify social forces.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:25.880
Technology and place and
culture are these very powerful

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:26.640
operating forces.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.090
 

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:34.100
When I make public
buildings, I'm

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:37.050
compelled to kind of dissolve
any sense of hierarchies

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:38.650
which are disconcerting.

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:40.160
That maybe comes
from my background,

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.020
being of multiple identities.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:47.170
So I'm sensitive to
how things play out.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:49.710
The National Museum of African
American History and Culture

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:51.740
is probably the most
significant project

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:54.560
I've engaged in in
my career so far,

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:59.980
and probably will be one of the
defining projects, I'm sure.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:03.850
This is a museum where the
container and the content

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:05.570
are one.

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:08.360
The museum's central
silhouette is

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:12.750
derived from a Yoruba
capital head, a wooden column,

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.940
which holds a porch
in a shrine house.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:20.110
This triple-headed
ziggurat was actually

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:25.040
a beautiful way of encapsulating
this universe of ideas.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.000
I have a deep interest in
Africa and Africa's diaspora.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.470
When I look at abstractions
from the continents,

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:35.970
I'm trying to understand how
abstraction was incredibly

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:40.410
successful in unifying
complex tribes and groups

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:44.500
to create ideas about larger
collective organizations.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:49.600
 

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.510
Culture is intrinsically
connected to fabric-- society

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:57.560
being a woven textile, a
system that's interconnected

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.080
and that the strength
of the society

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:02.332
is through that
interconnectedness.

00:02:02.332 --> 00:02:05.280
And I'm much more interested
in thinking about architecture

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.050
through this notion of fabric,
through interconnectedness,

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:09.835
and through handing
down of abstractions

00:02:09.835 --> 00:02:10.710
and interconnections.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:13.800
 

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.900
The Sugar Hill project,
where the rose embossing

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.390
was not literally drawn.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.885
It was a vector drawing,
which disappeared and appeared

00:02:20.885 --> 00:02:23.720
depending on the way that
the sunlight operated on it.

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:25.690
And the reason for it
was because the land

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:27.365
that we were occupying
was farmland.

00:02:27.365 --> 00:02:28.678
There were rose farms up there.

00:02:28.678 --> 00:02:31.370
 

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:34.170
Buildings can imbue within
them narratives about geography

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:37.530
or about people, without
being mimetic or figurative.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:40.950
It can use narrative to describe
what that abstraction could

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:42.930
be about.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.830
Public architecture
dissolves and becomes

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:49.200
less about form and becomes
more about scalelessness

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.950
and interaction.

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:52.940
And private architecture
increasingly

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:56.200
becomes about
havens and moments.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.760
So maybe from that,
you can imagine

00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:59.483
what my ideal campus might be.

00:02:59.483 --> 00:03:02.141
 

00:03:02.141 --> 00:03:05.640
I think that the campus has
a very interesting question.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.510
I think it asks the campus
to absolutely weld itself

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:12.660
with the public life
of the community.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:13.920
The MIT campus is interesting.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:17.100
That original building is
really powerful for students.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.150
The architecture of that
building and its history

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:21.173
and its layering
of other people who

00:03:21.173 --> 00:03:24.136
have been inspired
within that environment.

00:03:24.136 --> 00:03:26.480
Architecture can
dissolve itself by

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.840
dismantling certain formal,
commercial, and civic ideas,

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.460
which have to do sealed
palaces of information,

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:36.490
to becoming more like markets.

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:38.210
The notion of a
place of exchange

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:40.640
becomes maybe a very
interesting model

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:44.601
for a place of
education and learning.

00:03:44.601 --> 00:03:46.600
It's not so much about
the quantity of the books

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:48.099
anymore, because
actually that could

00:03:48.099 --> 00:03:49.770
be gotten through the internet.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:53.790
But the quality of the space
as a gathering, edifying space

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.570
is actually now very critical.

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:58.460
Because as creatures
of habit and pattern,

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:03.167
we actually like the idea
of places of simulation.

00:04:03.167 --> 00:04:05.000
I think in our century,
in the 21st century,

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:08.570
architecture is about having the
tools which give you the data,

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:11.890
but constantly reapplying the
tools in very precise ways

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:14.910
which creates new conditions
which amplify and help society.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:18.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:18.260 --> 00:05:12.539
 

